[{"result":{"data":{"json":{"id":"159","stub":{"id":"159","section":"Lower Run","altname":"","river":"Canyon Creek","difficulty":"IIItoIV","states":["USA-CAL"]},"primaryGaugeStatus":null,"pointOfInterests":[{"type":"hazard","description":"We portaged on river left","id":"FAoEdqgvlx9ukqWgotd87","location":{"latitude":"40.773532","longitude":"-123.050867"},"name":"River wide strainer","distance":"1.05","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false}],"alerts":[{"id":"k7F9gNSa5wC7KjgwIXyD3","title":"Wood hazards, Spring 2023","post_date":"2023-04-27 21:52:10+00","detail":"via Don Iverson post in Arcata Whitewater Kayakers\nWe did Canyon Creek last weekend. Two things to note--one is that the Dreamflows realtime gauge reading is waaay off...it said 1500 cfs, more like 150. Second is that there are about 10-15 new trees in the river, most of them riverwide, some in slightly sketchy places (e.g. around a blind corner). They were avoidable with some effort at low flows, but could be more hazardous as flows increase. Heads up.","user":{"name":"Jeff Venturino"}}],"photos":[],"detail":{"id":"159","permitUrl":null,"permitInfo":null,"description":"Canyon Creek is a tributary of the Trinity River.    The main run is short but the upper from the last bridge is kinda like an upper trinity/coffee type thing. Lots of 2 and 3 and great waves at higher flows.\n\nMany rapids on the run are on bends.   The current flows over a boulder bar (gravel bar?), pushes into a wall, then turns sharply.   The creek has a really good rapid in a mini gorge near the end.\n\nA GUIDE TO THE BEST WHITEWATER IN THE STATE OF CALIFORNIA, HOLBEK & STANLEY, 1988","averageGradient":"79","maxGradient":null,"length":"2.8","correlations":[],"pointOfInterestIDs":["FAoEdqgvlx9ukqWgotd87"],"geometry":{"type":"LineString","coordinates":[[-123.05783,40.784541],[-123.057947,40.78436],[-123.058022,40.784205],[-123.058044,40.784083],[-123.058097,40.783978],[-123.058097,40.78384],[-123.058065,40.783629],[-123.0580